# DRAFT pages: noindex, not in nav or sitemap. Exec'd at the end of build.py on the drafts branch only.
page("guides/7-november-deadline/","MTD 7 November deadline: what to send, and what happens if you're late","The 7 November 2026 Making Tax Digital quarterly update deadline explained: what the update covers, nil updates, and why there are no penalty points for late updates in 2026 to 2027.",
hero("Deadline · 7 November 2026","The 7 November <em>deadline</em>","What to send, what it covers, and what happens if you miss it.",
 f"if you're using MTD for Income Tax, send your quarterly update covering 6 April to 5 October by 7 November. HMRC won't give penalty points for late quarterly updates in 2026 to 2027, but you must send them before you can file your tax return. <a href='{QU}' rel='noopener'>Source: HMRC</a>.")+
prose(f"""<h2>Who this applies to</h2><p>Anyone already using MTD for Income Tax. That's mainly sole traders and landlords whose qualifying income was over £50,000 in 2024 to 2025, who should have started on 6 April 2026, plus anyone who volunteered. <a href="{ELIG}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>. If you're not sure, use our <a href="../../#checker">checker</a>.</p>
<h2>What the update covers</h2><p>It's the second update of the tax year. With standard update periods it covers <strong>6 April to 5 October</strong>; with calendar periods, <strong>1 April to 30 September</strong>. The deadline is 7 November either way. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<p>Each update covers the tax year so far, not just the last three months. So if you spot a mistake from the first quarter, you can correct it in your records and it flows through. You don't need to resend the earlier update. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>It's a summary, not a tax return</h2><p>Your software adds up your digital records into totals for each income and expense category. HMRC doesn't see individual receipts or invoices, and you don't need to make accounting or tax adjustments first. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>If you had no income</h2><p>You still send an update. HMRC says that if you haven't received any income or had any expenses in the period, you must still send your quarterly update to tell them. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>What happens if you're late</h2><p>For the 2026 to 2027 tax year, <strong>HMRC won't apply penalty points for late quarterly updates</strong>. You will still need to send every update before you can submit your tax return, and late tax returns do still get points. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<p>From 2027 to 2028, each late update gets a point, and four points means a £200 penalty. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>. So this year is a good time to get into the habit without the risk.</p>
<p>If you're volunteering for MTD, penalties don't apply to late quarterly updates, though penalty points still apply to late tax returns. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>When you can send it</h2><p>Any time from 6 October (or 1 October on calendar periods) up to 7 November. You can also send up to 10 days before the period ends if you don't expect any more transactions. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>Quick checklist</h2><ul><li>Records up to date to 5 October (or 30 September).</li><li>Check the totals your software shows you.</li><li>Send one update for each self-employment and property business.</li><li>Put the next date in your diary: 7 February 2027. Our <a href="../deadline-calendar/">deadline calendar</a> does it for you.</li></ul>
<p>Need software? Our <a href="../../finder/">software finder</a> lets you filter the products on <a href="{TOOL}" rel="noopener">HMRC's list of MTD for Income Tax software</a>.</p>
<p class="small">Facts checked against GOV.UK on {UPDATED}. General information, not tax advice.</p>"""),
faqs=[("When is the MTD quarterly update due in November 2026?",f"By 7 November 2026, for the period 6 April to 5 October (or 1 April to 30 September on calendar periods). <a href='{QU}'>HMRC</a>."),
("Is there a penalty for a late quarterly update in 2026 to 2027?",f"No penalty points for late quarterly updates in 2026 to 2027, but you must send them before you can submit your tax return. <a href='{QU}'>HMRC</a>."),
("Do I need to send an update if I had no income?",f"Yes. HMRC says you must still send it to tell them. <a href='{QU}'>HMRC</a>.")])

page("guides/30k-threshold-april-2027/","Do I need MTD at £30,000? Your April 2027 checklist","If your self-employment and property income was over £30,000 in 2025 to 2026, you'll need Making Tax Digital for Income Tax from 6 April 2027. What counts, what HMRC will do, and how to get ready.",
hero("April 2027","Over £30,000? <em>MTD from April 2027</em>","What counts towards the £30,000, what HMRC will do, and how to get ready in time.",
 f"if your qualifying income was over £30,000 in the 2025 to 2026 tax year, you'll need to use MTD for Income Tax from 6 April 2027. <a href='{ELIG}' rel='noopener'>Source: HMRC</a>.")+
prose(f"""<h2>What counts towards £30,000</h2><p>Your <strong>qualifying income</strong>: total self-employment and property income <em>before expenses</em>. Wages from a job (PAYE), dividends, pensions and partnership profit shares don't count. <a href="{QI}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<p>That means turnover, not profit. A sole trader turning over £34,000 with £15,000 profit is still over the line. HMRC's own example adds £25,000 of rent to £27,000 of self-employment income to get £52,000. <a href="{QI}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<p>If you own a rental jointly, only your share counts. <a href="{QI}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>Which year HMRC looks at</h2><p>The 2025 to 2026 tax year (6 April 2025 to 5 April 2026). HMRC reviews your Self Assessment return each year and, if you're over the threshold, writes to confirm you need to start by the following tax year. <a href="{ELIG}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>. The online return for 2025 to 2026 is due by 31 January 2027. <a href="{SADL}" rel="noopener">GOV.UK</a>.</p>
<p>Even if HMRC doesn't write to you, it's still your job to check and sign up. HMRC says it's your responsibility. <a href="{ELIG}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>Under £30,000?</h2><p>The next threshold is £20,000, based on 2026 to 2027 income, from 6 April 2028. <a href="{ELIG}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>. Some people are exempt, for example if they're digitally excluded. <a href="{ELIG}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>.</p>
<h2>Getting ready: a simple order</h2><ol class="checklist"><li>Check your 2025 to 2026 turnover and rent with our <a href="../../#checker">checker</a>.</li><li>Pick software that covers all your income sources. Compare every HMRC-recognised product in the <a href="../../finder/">finder</a>, or keep your spreadsheet with <a href="../bridging-software/">bridging software</a>.</li><li>Start keeping digital records early, so April isn't a cliff edge.</li><li>Sign up with HMRC. You need to be registered for Self Assessment and have sent a return in the last 2 years. <a href="{SIGN}" rel="noopener">HMRC: sign up</a>.</li><li>Diary your first deadline: 7 August 2027, for 6 April to 5 July. <a href="{QU}" rel="noopener">HMRC</a>. Our <a href="../deadline-calendar/">deadline calendar</a> has it.</li></ol>
<p class="small">Facts checked against GOV.UK on {UPDATED}. General information, not tax advice.</p>"""),
faqs=[("Is the £30,000 MTD threshold based on profit or turnover?",f"Turnover. Qualifying income is self-employment and property income before expenses. <a href='{QI}'>HMRC</a>."),
("When do I start MTD if I earned over £30,000 in 2025 to 2026?",f"From 6 April 2027. <a href='{ELIG}'>HMRC</a>."),
("Does my salary count towards the £30,000?",f"No. Employment (PAYE) income doesn't count towards qualifying income. <a href='{QI}'>HMRC</a>.")])
